1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned system that records every economic indicator, how it is computed, what assumptions it makes, and what its known limitations are. An economic indicator is a statistic about an economy: GDP, inflation, unemployment, the money supply, the balance of trade, the consumer price index, the purchasing managers' index, and many others. The Economic Indicator Provenance System records each indicator with its definition, its source data, its computation method, its assumptions, its revisions, its known limitations, and its provenance.

It reuses:

the Effective-Null Builder (App 11)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Instrument Provenance Registry (App 28)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for economists, policymakers, journalists, analysts, students, and anyone who uses economic indicators.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of economic indicators from users.

Store each indicator with its name, its definition, its source, its computation method, and its provenance.

Record the computation method: the formula, the data sources, the transformation.

Record the assumptions: what the indicator assumes about the economy.

Record the known limitations: what the indicator does not capture.

Record the revisions: how the indicator has changed over time, and why.

Record the base year or reference period.

Record the frequency: monthly, quarterly, annual, or other.

Record the seasonal adjustment: whether the indicator is seasonally adjusted, and by what method.

Record the coverage: the geographic and demographic scope.

Record the institutional source: the agency that publishes the indicator.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by indicator ID, name, source, frequency, base year, institutional source, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the definition, the computation method, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by source, by frequency, by institutional source, by year, by country.

Support exporting an indicator as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing an indicator from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking an indicator to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the indicator is described in a deposited work.

Support linking an indicator to a Numerical Verification Tool result if the indicator's values have been verified.

Support linking an indicator to an Instrument Provenance Registry record if the indicator's source data comes from a specific instrument.

Support the indicator map: a visualisation of indicators by country and by frequency.

Support the indicator revision timeline: a visualisation of how an indicator's definition has changed over time.

Support the indicator comparison view: a visualisation that compares two or more indicators for the same phenomenon.

Support the indicator assumption view: a visualisation of the assumptions and limitations of an indicator.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical indicator in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 indicators.

Versioned: the system records the STAG schema version, the economic indicator schema version, and the system version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

